    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "902319ba_ade0e85c",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 13,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-08T16:05:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "OK. But if DSW drops out we\u0027re at a loss in general... that ties in with my comments on the bug. Assuming EC is in DSW domain then we\u0027re likely ok since EC loses state too. But it\u0027s important to note our dependencies. Do we know that eSPI is in DSW domain too?",
      "parentUuid": "24dc2e08_57aa56d8",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e2309b1b_5b8704ee",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 13,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-08T19:10:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, the EC resides in DSW domain.\n\nAbout eSPI -- all the virtual signals reside in DSW power well and are reset by DSW_PWROK. From the spec, it seems like the eSPI master controller also should be residing in DSW domain, because that is the first one to come out of reset.",
      "parentUuid": "902319ba_ade0e85c",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bbf50d33_aa2aa8c5",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/espi.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 545,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-08T02:19:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Presumably that whatever power domain failed came back such that eSPi can communicate? Basically, do we have any recourse where eSPI communication completely fails and the EC thinks we\u0027re sitting in S0 when we aren\u0027t?",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6f9911b6_67607db4",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/espi.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 545,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-08T07:29:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "According to the spec, any unplanned reset event in host/PCH will result in an abrupt assertion of eSPI_Reset#, thus prematurely terminating any eSPI transactions that were going on. Also, the motivation behind adding a dedicated reset pin is to handle all kinds of global reset events and thus it acts as the fundamental reset to the eSPI interface.\n\nMy understanding of the spec is that no matter what component fails, eSPI_Reset# should always be asserted to the EC.",
      "parentUuid": "bbf50d33_aa2aa8c5",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
