    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "921cc968_d1c30148",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/espi.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 548,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-08T02:19:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is there a better way to kick the state machines? Can we have a fake bit which would allow us to clear it and let the power task get kicked from the event. That way we aren\u0027t running things from different tasks -- let lone interrupt handlers.",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8750ec7f_57750323",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/espi.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 548,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-08T07:29:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree it would be cleaner to kick the state machine without affecting any of its states directly. But the problem that I encountered was:\n1. This flag cannot be added as a power signal since all power signals are treated as gpios which are read to identify the value\n2. If we decide to simply add a global flag, I believe it would have to go in power/skylake.c (or to generalize in power/\u003cchipset\u003e.c) that will have to implement a function like chipset_handle_espi_reset(). For skylake, this will simply release the power button press and let the rest of state machine handle things on its own. But this chipset_handle_espi_reset will have to be provided by all chipsets that want to handle the espi_reset.\n\nDoes #2 above sound like a safer way?",
      "parentUuid": "921cc968_d1c30148",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "79f33858_8b9229a6",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/espi.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 548,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-08T16:05:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think we can do the virtual power signalling in common/power.c. By having a concept of a virtual state? It would not be a part of POWER_SIGNAL_COUNT. It would be a manipulation of in_signals. The calls can manipulate the state of the virtual signal signal by clearing/setting it in the right place. I think we could likely make it generic in some manner.\n\nIf manipulating the power signal mask is too much of a pain then you would still need the same logic for bailing out of a state. But that could be from a call to power_signal_interrupt().  Don\u0027t we just need to manipulate the state of the eSPI virtual wires and kick the chipset task? espi_vw_get_wire() needs to return asserted values for VW_SLP_S3_L and VW_SLP_S4_L. Can we not do that?",
      "parentUuid": "8750ec7f_57750323",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cffa8fdf_30e6cb61",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/espi.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 548,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-08T19:10:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So the problem here is that, when global reset event occurs, eSPI VW messages are not transmitted and so the slave does not know that the status of VW SLP_* signals has changed. espi_vw_get_wire() would still read the old values for all the SLP_* signals. However, once eSPI_Reset# is deasserted, PCH eSPI master controller sends the udpated VW SLP_* signals to the slave. This causes the slave (EC) to transition properly according to the received signals.\n\nThus, my initial thought was to just let the EC identify that there was a global reset event and take any necessary action i.e. in case of skylake release the power button that was being pressed. Once the eSPI_Reset# is de-asserted, PCH power signals would be re-sent by the master and cause appropriate transitions on the EC side.\n\nDo you think this could possess a problem if the PCH eSPI controller never gets out of reset? I am not sure how likely that condition is, because eSPI seems to come out of reset before anything else.",
      "parentUuid": "79f33858_8b9229a6",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ce84d641_4d2f9de1",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/espi.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 548,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-08T19:13:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In the second patchset, I have changed the logic to add a callback chipset_handle_espi_reset_assert() which basically allows chipset to take only the necessary action whenever global reset event occurs. Rest should still flow through the power state machine without us introducing any new path in it.",
      "parentUuid": "cffa8fdf_30e6cb61",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a6a83511_ebb26a29",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/espi.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 548,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-08T19:39:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e So the problem here is that, when global reset event occurs, eSPI VW messages are not transmitted and so the slave does not know that the status of VW SLP_* signals has changed. espi_vw_get_wire() would still read the old values for all the SLP_* signals. However, once eSPI_Reset# is deasserted, PCH eSPI master controller sends the udpated VW SLP_* signals to the slave. This causes the slave (EC) to transition properly according to the received signals.\n\u003e \n\u003e Thus, my initial thought was to just let the EC identify that there was a global reset event and take any necessary action i.e. in case of skylake release the power button that was being pressed. Once the eSPI_Reset# is de-asserted, PCH power signals would be re-sent by the master and cause appropriate transitions on the EC side.\n\nBut can\u0027t we override the status of the virtual wires in those registers? That\u0027s what I\u0027m suggesting. Clearing out the state in some manner such that those values reflect the condition you\u0027re trying to detect. That along with kicking the chipset task is sufficient for it to notice, no? Is it really not possible to override the signal status? \n\n\u003e \n\u003e Do you think this could possess a problem if the PCH eSPI controller never gets out of reset? I am not sure how likely that condition is, because eSPI seems to come out of reset before anything else.\n\nAny condition where the synchronous eSPI signalling is affected is potential to screw up the current state machine.",
      "parentUuid": "ce84d641_4d2f9de1",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d6956c2b_a53871c2",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/espi.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 548,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-08T20:40:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e But can\u0027t we override the status of the virtual wires in those registers? That\u0027s what I\u0027m suggesting. Clearing out the state in some manner such that those values reflect the condition you\u0027re trying to detect. That along with kicking the chipset task is sufficient for it to notice, no? Is it really not possible to override the signal status? \n\nFrom NPCX datasheet, it looks like the register bits for VW signal from master to slave can only be set by the master i.e. for the EC these register bits are RO. Thus, there is no way for the EC to mark the bits in the VWEVSMn register as 0. Michal/ML from Nuvoton can correct me if there is some way to do this other than setting the bits in VMEVSMn register.\n\n\u003e Any condition where the synchronous eSPI signalling is affected is potential to screw up the current state machine.\n\nThat is true. But if eSPI_Reset# is assured to be a signal which always indicates the state of eSPI communication, then EC will have to rely on that to make the right decision. Also, if eSPI_Reset# is never de-asserted then we might have bigger problems since there won\u0027t be any communication between EC and AP. Thus, it is important to make sure that EC never holds up the AP in a way that eSPI_Reset# cannot be deasserted. That would basically lead to a dead system.",
      "parentUuid": "a6a83511_ebb26a29",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d85db284_7b15efe7",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/espi.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 548,
      "author": {
        "id": 1165254
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-09T15:43:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e \u003e But can\u0027t we override the status of the virtual wires in those\n \u003e registers? That\u0027s what I\u0027m suggesting. Clearing out the state in\n \u003e some manner such that those values reflect the condition you\u0027re\n \u003e trying to detect. That along with kicking the chipset task is\n \u003e sufficient for it to notice, no? Is it really not possible to\n \u003e override the signal status?\n \u003e \n \u003e From NPCX datasheet, it looks like the register bits for VW signal\n \u003e from master to slave can only be set by the master i.e. for the EC\n \u003e these register bits are RO. Thus, there is no way for the EC to\n \u003e mark the bits in the VWEVSMn register as 0. Michal/ML from Nuvoton\n \u003e can correct me if there is some way to do this other than setting\n \u003e the bits in VMEVSMn register.\n \u003e \n \n\nThe only way would be that the firmware will create its own VW SLP_* bits/variables that will be updated per the VWEVSMn register and also on eSPI# global reset. \nBut if the only issue is the apshutdown, I assume it is not worth to effort to make this modification.",
      "parentUuid": "d6956c2b_a53871c2",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3039187c_5678ff1a",
        "filename": "chip/npcx/espi.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 548,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-02-09T15:50:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I can envision needing that very thing in the future. We\u0027re just using forcing_shutdown as a proxy for when it\u0027s appropriate to stop pressing power button. That was keeping the chipset from continuing forward and updating the virtual wires. I don\u0027t think we\u0027ll always be so lucky depending on the SoC/platform behavior.",
      "parentUuid": "d85db284_7b15efe7",
      "revId": "02a8e6dd174e2c92bd1044b6b949e7bde706b18a",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
